yes/no and multiple-choice questions for https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B

    llm.decide(state, "did the deployment succeed?", ["yes", "no", "unclear"])
    -> {"yes": 0.91, "no": 0.03, "unclear": 0.06}

the usual way to get a typed answer out of a chat model is a json
grammar. ask for {"enum": ["yes", "no", "unclear"]} and watch what
the model actually does to say yes:

    forward pass 1: prompt          -> grammar allows only "   -> sample "
    forward pass 2: prompt "        -> grammar allows y n u   -> sample yes
    forward pass 3: prompt "yes     -> grammar allows only "  -> sample "
    forward pass 4: prompt "yes"    -> grammar allows only eos
    json.loads('"yes"')             -> "yes"

four passes through 27B parameters. three of them produce tokens
the grammar had already decided. at the end you hold one sampled
string and no idea whether the model was 91% sure or 51% sure.

now look at pass 1 again. the model already knew the answer there.
its logits for y, n and u were sitting in that output. this repo
just reads them:

    forward pass 1: prompt ending in "answer with the letter:"
    logits[A], logits[B], logits[C]  ->  softmax  ->  done

one pass, no sampling, no grammar, no parsing, and you get the
whole distribution instead of one draw from it. ask 21 questions
about the same document and the document is prefilled once, the
21 short suffixes run as one batch.

what it costs: the answer is read at the first token, so the model
cannot think first. this is qwen's gut reaction. for a decision
that needs reasoning or an output that is not a small fixed set
of options, use chat with a grammar.

https://github.com/TheoLeeCJ/openjev did this with a 4B model.

quick demo:

    $ ./run.sh uv run example.py
